VALUES (
  'INSIDE THE ROW — VOL. 3',
  'VOL. 3',
  2026,
  E'THE FOUNDERS ROW LONGEVITY FUND\n\nInvestment Memorandum\n\n\nThe Moment\n\nSomething fundamental is shifting. For the first time in history, a generation expects to live past 100—not as an outlier, but as a baseline. This belief is grounded in science: we can train VO₂ max, optimize metabolic and hormonal health, and we know that community and purpose extend life as powerfully as medicine.\n\nAs a result, a 20-year-old isn''t planning for retirement, but for multiple lives—a second act at 60, a third at 80—investing in health span, not just lifespan, for decades.\n\nThe longevity economy is projected to exceed $600 billion by 2030, yet most capital is chasing biotech, Pharma, and diagnostics.\n\nWe see the opportunity in the infrastructure and behavioral layer—the studios, recovery centers, wellness hotels, concierge clinics, and community spaces where longevity is actually lived. The question isn''t whether longevity is the opportunity, but who will build the world around it.\n\n\nThe Gap\n\nPrivate equity built the modern wellness industry. Over the last 25 years, PE capital professionalized fitness, scaled boutique concepts into national brands, and created billions in enterprise value. The playbook worked, and the returns were real.\n\nBut that success created its own constraint. As fund sizes grew, the math changed. A $500 million fund can''t deploy $3 million checks without impact. A $1 billion fund needs even larger deals to matter. The result was an inevitable move upmarket—later-stage, larger transactions—and early stage was left behind.\n\nYet early stage is where the greatest returns are made. Orangetheory was studio #1 before becoming a 1,500-location brand. SweatHouz was a single prototype before scaling to 200 studios. Category-defining winners—the ones that delivered 5x, 7x, even 10x returns—were built early, when concepts were unproven and checks were small.\n\nToday, that dynamic has created a widening gap. Private equity has too much capital chasing too few later-stage deals, while exceptional early-stage founders in wellness and longevity struggle to find partners who understand their businesses, add real operational value, and structure aligned deals.\n\nThat''s the gap we fill. Founders Row is the small-cap/early stage operating partner for health, wellness, and longevity. We bring PE-level operational expertise to opportunities too small for PE to touch. We don''t strip founder DNA—we amplify it. We build companies the right way, then hand them to private equity when they''re ready.\n\nWe''re not competing with PE. We''re their farm system. With so much capital chasing deals, PE firms have to buy—and we''re building exactly what they need.\n\n\nJamie Weeks\n\nI began my career at UBS Brinson Partners, where their landmark study showed that 91.5% of investment returns are derived from asset allocation—not stock picking or timing. That insight shaped everything that followed.\n\nI spent eleven years at UBS Brinson Partners as a top-performing wholesaler, advising family offices and entrepreneurs on capital allocation and deal structure. I then became a family wealth Director at Morgan Stanley where I learned that being an entrepreneur was my future.\n\nIn 2014, I built studio #140 in Orangetheory Fitness, I then built the largest franchise operation in the system, and sold to JW Childs in 2017—delivering a 7.4x average return to seed investors in 36 months. In 2019, I founded SweatHouz when contrast therapy was fringe, and sold it three years later for an average return of 7x for my seed investors in 27 months, and remain the largest individual owner as it scales to 200+ studios at the end of 2026.\n\nTwo companies. Two exits. Category-defining outcomes—by getting in early, building with discipline, and handing off to PE. In September 2025, I launched Founders Row to systematize that playbook—staying early stage, where returns are highest and the operator first mentality benefits the founder.\n\n\nStaying Ahead of the Curve\n\nWe''ve built our track record by seeing what''s next—boutique fitness in 2014, contrast therapy in 2019. Today, we''re already positioned across additional emerging categories that will define the future of health and wellness:\n\n• Digital Psychedelics — Dream Digital, Tersa, Morpheus—vibration, light, and sound for therapeutic mental health benefits without pharmaceuticals. We''re building the category.\n\n• Wellness Hospitality — We have been asked to co-develop a health/wellness hotel featuring The Sky Glass Garden—rooftop greenhouse, organic café, nutritional programming. People want more spaces to be health forward.\n\n• Peptides & Preventative Medicine — Companies like Fit9 Wellness, RegenMD and other concierge medicine platforms making advanced protocols accessible to the everyday person. This isn''t slowing down, it''s moving at a lightning pace.\n\n\nThe Ecosystem\n\nSweatHouz (recovery), Sculpthouse (cardio/flexibility), Revstrong (strength), The Baking Social (nutrition/community). Asset allocation that matches the lives of the next generation of centenarians.\n\n\nOur Edge\n\nFounders are already giving us equity just to advise them. We''re inside these businesses before we write a check—understanding operations, team, and unit economics. We bring the playbook that built Orangetheory and SweatHouz—plus the team that helped write it:\n\n• Tori Woodhull, Chief of Staff — 10 years with me. Runs the firm day to day.\n• Jess Yuan, Chief Operating Officer — With me in the early OTF days. 12+ years in consumer private equity.\n• Mike Mehr, Chief Development Officer — Scaled from 1 → 142 Orangetheory locations.\n• Tori Harris, Brand Marketing — Was Head of Influencer Marketing at SweatHouz.\n• Kevin Bite, Head of AI Implementation — For all brands.\n\n\nThe Thesis: Longevity… is the same as Asset Allocation\n\nWe invest parallel to the life of a centenarian. Our focus is the next generation—the 20-year-old today who has a real chance to live past 100. Where they will spend their health and wellness dollars over the next 80 years is our core investment thesis.\n\nWhat we know is this: centenarians don''t reach 100 by focusing on one thing. They don''t just do cardio, eat well, or sleep more. They are diversified—across movement, strength, nutrition, sleep, recovery, mental health, community, and preventative medicine—allocating across the full spectrum of inputs that drive longevity.\n\nAt UBS, I learned that 91.5% of investment returns are due to asset allocation. The same principle applies to longevity. You can''t optimize health span by going all-in on one input while ignoring the rest.\n\nThat''s the pie chart of longevity. And the Founders Row Longevity Fund aims to match it:\n\n• Cardio — VO2 max—the strongest predictor of all-cause mortality\n• Strength — Muscle mass, bone density, and metabolic health\n• Mental Health — Stress, cognition, and emotional resilience\n• Nutrition — Clean ingredients, protein optimization, and behavior change\n• Sleep — The recovery/longevity multiplier\n• Recovery — Infrared, cold exposure, and compression\n• Medicine — Peptides, hormone optimization, and concierge care\n• Community — Loneliness is a mortality risk equal to smoking, read that again.\n• Flexibility — Functional movement, and injury prevention\n• Testing — VO2 max, biomarkers, continuous monitoring\n\nPeople who want to live to 100, 110, 120 can''t focus on one slice. They need the whole pie. We invest across all of it.\n\n\nThe Fund\n\nFounders Row Longevity Fund I\n\n$50 million deployed across 20+ early-stage investments over 24–36 months. Diversified across the longevity pie chart. Built to return capital faster.\n\nEarly stage means we move the needle faster. We''re not waiting 7–10 years for exits. We built and sold twice in under 3 years each. By creating value faster at this stage, we expect to return capital faster as well.\n\nWe''re building the infrastructure of the longevity economy. Staying ahead of the curve. Creating companies PE will want to buy. Returning capital to investors faster. One founder & brand at a time.\n\n— Jamie Weeks\n\nFounder & Managing Partner\n\nFounders Row\n\nAtlanta, GA',
  true,
  NOW()
);

